 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">1. Where B-grade and rebuilt cells come from</h2>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">What cell grading actually means</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Every large cell manufacturer runs a grading line at the end of production. Cells that fail the first screen, on capacity deviation, internal resistance, or self-discharge, are downgraded to B-grade and sold at a discount into secondary markets. Nothing about a B-grade cell is hidden: wholesalers list them openly as "Grade B LiFePO4 / Li-ion cells". The fraud happens one step later, when a pack assembler buys them, re-wraps them, and quotes them to you as Grade A (per industry grading conventions, not a standardized rating system).</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">The rebuilt-cell economy</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A rebuilt cell is a used cell re-wrapped to look new, fresh film hides wear, a reprinted date code hides age.: it is a cell with history, pulled from rejected packs, recovered returns, or production overflow, cleaned, re-wrapped in fresh shrink film, and re-labelled with a new date code. Fresh film hides wear; a reprinted date code hides age. On the quote sheet both look identical, which is why quote-stage verification has to rest on documents and measurements, not on photographs.</p>

 <table class="w-full text-sm mb-6">
 <thead class="bg-brandBlue text-white">
 <tr><th class="p-3 text-left">Signal</th><th class="p-3 text-left">Grade A (first-run)</th><th class="p-3 text-left">B-grade / rebuilt</th></tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody class="bg-white">
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200"><td class="p-3">Quote level (10,000mAh, 500 pcs)</td><td class="p-3">$5.80-8.00 FOB</td><td class="p-3">15-25% below Grade-A</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200"><td class="p-3">Cell documentation</td><td class="p-3">Brand, model, datasheet on request</td><td class="p-3">"High quality", no model number</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200"><td class="p-3">Batch traceability</td><td class="p-3">Date codes match packaging</td><td class="p-3">Reprinted codes, mixed batches</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200"><td class="p-3">Field return rate</td><td class="p-3">&lt;0.5%</td><td class="p-3">5-8%</td></tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>

 <div class="bg-brandBlue/5 border-l-4 border-brandOrange rounded-r-xl p-6 mt-6">
 <p class="text-[11px] font-black text-brandOrange uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Expert Insight</p>
 <blockquote class="text-slate-700 text-base italic leading-relaxed">"Nobody in the supply chain advertises a rebuilt cell, the incentive runs the other way. Every verification step has to be one the supplier cannot fake with photos: documents with model numbers, sample units you test yourself, and reports that name the exact cell inside your pack."</blockquote>
 <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-2">, Nina Nico, Global Procurement &amp; Sourcing Manager, WOWOHCOOL</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

