 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">3. The discharge test that catches re-celled batches</h2>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Measure delivered capacity, not claimed capacity</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Delivered capacity at the 5V output, not the rated cell figure, is what your buyer experiences. Delivered mAh is always lower than the cell figure because of conversion losses; that gap is normal. What exposes rebuilt cells is the pattern across units and across cycles: Grade-A cells cluster tightly within datasheet tolerance; re-wrapped used cells scatter widely and fade from the first repeated cycle.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">The four-step protocol</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Five units, three cycles, one datasheet: the discharge protocol takes an afternoon and turns cell grade from a claim into a measurement.</p>
 <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
 <li><strong>Sample five units</strong> from the supplier's stock batch, not from a hand-picked demo box.</li>
 <li><strong>Charge and discharge at rated current</strong> through the 5V output, logging delivered mAh per unit.</li>
 <li><strong>Repeat for three cycles</strong>, rebuilt cells lose measurable capacity between cycles; first-run cells do not.</li>
 <li><strong>Compare against the datasheet</strong>, a spread wider than the datasheet tolerance band, or visible fade by cycle three, ends the conversation.</li>
 </ol>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">In the case that opened this article, the delivered figure came in at 17,100mAh against a 20,000mAh rating, 85.5% delivered before conversion losses were even considered, with visible fade by cycle three. That is the rebuilt-cell signature: not one bad number, but a bad pattern.</p>

 <div class="mt-6 mb-6">
 <img src="/image/blog/power-bank/power-bank-current-voltage-test.webp" alt="Discharge testing a power bank sample on a QC bench: measuring delivered mAh to verify cell grade before OEM production" loading="lazy" class="max-w-3xl mx-auto rounded-2xl shadow-lg w-full">
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== [10] H2 4: UN38.3 ===== -->
<section id="un383" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">4. UN38.3 and documentation cross-checks</h2>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">One report, one cell type, match all three layers</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">UN38.3 is the transport safety test every lithium cell must pass before air or sea shipment, and a report costs $1,000-2,500 for a specific cell type. That specificity is what makes it a verification tool: the report names the exact cell model tested. Cross-check three layers, the cell model on the report, the cells physically inside your samples (open one unit if the volume justifies it), and the batch date codes. All three must name the same cell.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">The mismatch patterns that matter</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Two patterns recur in the field. The first is a borrowed report: the factory shows a genuine UN38.3 document for a well-known cell while the pack contains something cheaper, the paperwork describes a cell you are not buying. The second is a report for an unidentifiable lab, which cannot be traced if customs or your marketplace platform asks. Both fail the same way under scrutiny: ask which laboratory issued the report and which cell model it covers, then compare with what ships. For the transport-side rules your shipment will face next, our guide to dangerous goods packaging certification walks through the full compliance chain.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== [11] H2 5: Cost ===== -->
<section id="cost" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">5. What B-grade actually costs you</h2>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">The return-rate arithmetic</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">For a 2,000-unit batch of 10,000mAh units, the B-grade saving on the quote is roughly $2,400 (15-25% off a $5.80-8.00 Grade-A band). Processing a 5-8% return rate means handling 100-160 defective units: return freight, triage, refunds or replacements, and the review damage on a marketplace listing. At realistic handling costs the return bill alone is several times the quote saving, before counting the warranty obligation and the listing's conversion drop.</p>

 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">A trajectory built on Grade-A cells</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">From 500 units to 10,000+ annually in two years, at a 4.5-star rating across 900+ reviews. The total entry cost was $8,000-18,000, product, branding, certification, and the first batch included. The scaling worked because the return rate stayed near 0.5% instead of eating the margin: cell grade is not a detail of the BOM, it is the assumption the whole business model rests on.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

