<!-- ===== [13] FAQ ===== -->
<section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What are B-grade lithium cells?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">B-grade cells failed their maker's first quality screen, on capacity deviation, internal resistance, or self-discharge, and are sold at a discount to secondary markets. Rebuilt cells go one step further: they are used or rejected cells re-wrapped to look new.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How much cheaper are B-grade cells in a power bank quote?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">15-25% below Grade-A pricing: a 10,000mAh standard unit quotes at $4.35-6.80 instead of $5.80-8.00 per unit at 500 pieces. The saving is smaller than the cost of processing a 5-8% field return rate.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Can a discharge test really expose rebuilt cells?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Yes. Discharging five sample units at rated current and measuring delivered mAh against the rated figure exposes two patterns at once: capacity below spec, and fast fade between repeated cycles. Grade-A cells hold within their datasheet tolerance; rebuilt cells drop measurably from cycle one.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What should I check in the UN38.3 report?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Three things: the exact cell model named, the batch and date codes matching your samples, and an identifiable testing laboratory. A UN38.3 report costs $1,000-2,500 and covers one cell type, a mismatched report means the paperwork describes a different cell than the one shipped.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

